  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nI know you wish you could read something new and interesting, and I \nknow I wish I could write something new and interesting, but I want \nto be honest: nothing has really changed. High pressure remains \ncentered near the Four Corners region, meandering south-southeast \nthrough the week, keeping us hot and dry through the end of the \nweek. There is a very low chance of some seabreeze convection in the \nafternoons, but most locations will not be lucky enough to see this. \nGuidance is still hinting at the potential for a disturbance to \nsneak around the eastern periphery of the ridge around the Friday \ntimeframe. This could lead to some isolated showers and slightly \ncooler temperatures but, again, this is still pretty far out so we \nare not getting our hopes up.\n\nIt is going to continue to be miserably hot for this upcoming week \nwith high temperatures in the 90s along the coast to around 105 \ninland. Expect we will continue with below normal moisture today and \ntomorrow which will help keep heat index values below 110 for most \nof South Texas. Because of this, there is not a Heat Advisory \ncurrently issued for today, but we may need to issue a short-fused \nadvisory this afternoon. Moisture begins a gradual return Wednesday \nthrough the end of the week, resulting in higher heat index values \nonce again. Heat Advisories will likely be needed Wednesday through \nthe end of the week. Regardless of the slightly lower heat index \nvalues, we urge you all to be extremely cautious if you are outside. \nCumulative heat stress can lead to increased chances for heat \nexhaustion or heat stroke. Remember to take breaks in the shade or \nair conditioning, drink water/electrolytes, and pace yourself if \nworking outside. Don't leave children or pets in your vehicle, even \nfor short periods of time.",
